Let $\mathbf{q}$ denote the foot of the perpendicular from $\mathbf{p}$ to the line.  Then $\mathbf{q}$ lies in the intersection of the plane with normal vector $\mathbf{v} - \mathbf{p}$ passing through $\mathbf{p},$ and the plane that contains the line, and the direction of the line.  The direction of the line is parallel to $\mathbf{d},$ so this line can be parameterized by
\[\begin{pmatrix} 4 + (-2)t \\ -6t \\ 1 - 3t \end{pmatrix}.\]Note that
\[\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} + (-2) \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + 6 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.\]This means that any line going through the point $(3,-3,3)$ and parallel to $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 0 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}$ also passes through the origin.

Thus, letting $\mathbf{a}$ be the position vector of the point of the line corresponding to $t = 0,$
\[\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix},\]and letting $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -3 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix},\]we can take $\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{p} - \mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{d} = \arctan \frac{\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}}{\|\mathbf{p} - \mathbf{a}\|}.$

Since these are perpendicular, $\overrightarrow{\mathbf{p} - \mathbf{a}} \cdot \overrightarrow{\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}} = 0,$ so $(\mathbf{p} - \mathbf{a}) \cdot (\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) = 0.$  Substituting,
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = 2 - 9 + 6 = 0.\]Hence, $\mathbf{v}$ would be the answer we seek.

Then
